<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edmund Burke</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1729">1729</a>–<a href="/years/1797">1797</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-burgh">House of Burgh</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edmund Burke was an Irish-born statesman, orator and political thinker, educated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinity-college-dublin">Trinity College Dublin</a>. As a Whig member of the British Parliament he supported conciliation with the American colonies and relief for Irish Catholics, and he led the impeachment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warren-hastings">Warren Hastings</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reflections-on-the-revolution-in-france">Reflections on the Revolution in France</a> of 1790 made him a founding figure of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conservatism">conservatism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edmund-burke.jpg" alt="Edmund Burke" width="320" height="370" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Joshua_Reynolds_-_Edmund_Burke,_1729_-_1797._Statesman,_orator_and_author_-_PG_2362_-_National_Galleries_of_Scotland_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Protestant Ireland produced a remarkable intellectual culture: Trinity College Dublin was founded in 1592, and Jonathan Swift, George Berkeley, Oliver Goldsmith and <b>Edmund Burke</b> were among its writers and thinkers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ireland">Kingdom of Ireland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Edmund Burke</b>’s Reflections on the Revolution in France of 1790 blamed abstract theorising for the destruction of a traditional order, and the Terror seemed to many to confirm his warning: Condorcet, who wrote his Sketch for a Historical Picture of the Progress of the Human Mind while in…</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>Jean-Jacques Rousseau’s novel Julie, or the New Heloise of 1761 made feeling and natural scenery central to literature, and <b>Edmund Burke</b>’s Philosophical Enquiry of 1757 distinguished the beautiful from the sublime, the thrill of awe before storms, mountains and the infinite.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Edmund Burke</b> defended a “natural aristocracy” of men raised to public responsibility, and property handed down within families, as sources of continuity and prudence against revolutionary experiment.</blockquote><a href="/government/aristocracy">Aristocracy · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Edmund Burke</b>, in his Reflections on the Revolution in France (1790), defended the Revolution of 1688 as “made to preserve our ancient indisputable laws and liberties”, a model of change that kept inherited institutions.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Edmund Burke</b> had defended party in 1770 as a body of men united on a shared principle, and Bagehot gave the mature system its classic description.</blockquote><a href="/government/parliamentary-democracy">Parliamentary democracy · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. Died in Beaconsfield, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.35,-6.26&amp;year=1773" aria-label="Dublin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4411 329 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Edmund Burke"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4861 599h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4861 599h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.35,-6.26&amp;year=1773">What was here in 1773? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
